## 3. Solution strategy

A **modular monolith** (ADR-0001): one API deployable with eight capability modules and one PostgreSQL database, plus a static single-page web app. It beats the next-simpler style (a layered monolith without module boundaries) because R2 modules (MOD-06, MOD-08) ship after the MVP and a third clinic must arrive as data (QA-011); it beats microservices because the two drivers need one transaction, not a saga (QA-003), and the team is small with a firm date (CON-001).

| Pattern | Driver that needs it | Where |
|---|---|---|
| Layers (entry, business, data) in the one deployable | Always | ctr-clinic-api |
| Database exclusion constraint on overlapping appointments (ADR-0003) | QA-003 | ent-appointment in ctr-db |
| Message rows with a precomputed send time, claimed by a dispatcher (ADR-0002, ADR-0009) | QA-004 | cmp-messaging, ent-message |
| Ports and adapters (ADR-0006), one adapter per external provider | QA-009, QA-004: two providers, one core | cmp-channel-adapters |
| Retry with backoff, timeout, circuit breaker on provider calls | QA-004, QA-005 | cmp-channel-adapters |
| Idempotent webhooks and idempotent sends | QA-004 | cmp-messaging, cmp-channel-adapters |
| Role-based access with clinic scoping in each module | QA-007 | cmp-identity and every module |
| Account and person split (ADR-0008) | FR-006, FR-025, PD-007 | ent-patient-account, ent-patient |
| Static CDN delivery, cookie session (ADR-0004) | QA-001, QA-010, QA-007 | ctr-web |

Not used, and why: no message broker (volume about 1,000 reminders a week, ADR-0002), no cache (live queries meet QA-001 at this size), no API gateway (one deployable), no CQRS or event sourcing (the history table in FR-024 is an audit log, not the source of state), no Kubernetes (ADR-0005).

