internet -->|HTTPS| node_cdn
node_cdn -->|"/api HTTPS"| node_api_a
node_cdn -->|"/api HTTPS"| node_api_b
node_api_a -->|SQL TLS| node_db_primary
node_api_b -->|SQL TLS| node_db_primary
node_db_primary -->|sync replication| node_db_standby
node_secrets -.->|env vars at start| node_api_a
node_secrets -.->|env vars at start| node_api_b
```

CI/CD: per commit, build → unit tests → contract tests against `contracts/clinic-api-v1.openapi.yaml` → image scan → deploy to staging → smoke test (health, sign-in with test code, book and cancel against sandbox providers) → deploy to prod by rolling replacement, one replica at a time behind the health check. Database changes are forward-only migrations that stay compatible with the previous release (expand, then contract). The web app is built and published separately, with the previous build kept. Rollback trigger: `GET /health` fails, 5xx rate above 2% for 5 minutes, or reminder lag above 15 minutes after a deploy; rollback is redeploying the previous image and the previous static build. Releases: MVP for 1 March 2027, R2 about 2–3 weeks later; R2 screens are hidden by a feature flag until their data is ready.

## 9. Security

### Trust boundaries
| ID | Boundary | Members |
|---|---|---|
| tb-internet | Users and external systems | act-patient, act-receptionist, act-dentist, act-practice-manager, act-sms-provider, act-whatsapp-provider, browsers running ctr-web |
| tb-dmz | Edge | CDN, load balancer and WAF in front of ctr-web and ctr-clinic-api (managed edge node) |
| tb-internal | Application | ctr-clinic-api |
| tb-data | Data stores | ctr-db |

Identity and access: patients sign in by phone code (FR-002) and staff by username and password (FR-003); both receive a signed JWT in an `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite=Strict` cookie (ASM-008). The token carries user id and role only; the role, clinic and active flag are re-read from the database on each request, so deactivation (FR-005) acts at once. Authorisation is RBAC with clinic scoping (FR-004, NFR-006), enforced in each module through `cmp-identity`'s access context, not in the web app. Staff passwords: at least 10 characters, hashed with argon2id, lockout after 5 failures for 15 minutes. Machine clients: the two providers call webhooks signed with a shared secret. There is no service-to-service traffic: one deployable.

Data protection and secrets: TLS on every edge and to the database; encryption at rest on the database, snapshots and backups; secrets (database credentials, provider keys, webhook secrets, token signing key) in the cloud secret store, passed as environment variables, never in images or documents. PII (names, phone numbers, birth years) is never written to logs; logs carry ids and a correlation id. Rendered message text is stored for 90 days only (ASM-012). Messages to providers hold only a phone number and the template values (clinic, dentist, date, time, link), never treatment notes. Erasure on request (NFR-007) is in `cmp-identity` (ASM-013).

### Threats (STRIDE)
| ID | STRIDE | Target (rel or container id) | Threat | Mitigation | Status (mitigated / accepted + RSK id) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| thr-001 | Spoofing | rel-005 | Guessing a patient's sign-in code | 6-digit code, stored hashed, valid 10 minutes, 5 tries then 15-minute lock per phone, per-IP throttle (cmp-identity) | mitigated |
| thr-002 | Spoofing | rel-005 | Staff password guessing or credential stuffing | argon2id, 10-character minimum, 5-failure lockout, WAF rate limit | mitigated |
| thr-003 | Spoofing | rel-005 | Someone with a SIM swap or a recycled number signs in as that patient | Code limits only; data exposed is name and appointments (ADR-0007) | accepted (RSK-009) |
| thr-004 | Tampering | rel-005 | A user alters ids (patient, clinic) in a request to act on others' records | Server-side authorisation from the token's owner on every operation, never from request ids alone | mitigated |
| thr-005 | Tampering | rel-005 | Forged cross-site request using the session cookie | `SameSite=Strict`, CSRF header and Origin check on writes (ADR-0004) | mitigated |
| thr-006 | Repudiation | rel-005 | A booking change is denied ("I never cancelled") | ent-appointment-event records who, what and when for every change (FR-024) | mitigated |
| thr-007 | Information disclosure | rel-005 | One clinic's receptionist or another patient reads data they should not | RBAC and clinic scoping in every module; automated tests per role for each operation (QA-007) | mitigated |
| thr-008 | Denial of service | rel-005 | Flood of code requests causes SMS cost and blocks real patients | Per-phone and per-IP limits, daily cap on codes, WAF rules; residual cost accepted | accepted (RSK-002) |
| thr-009 | Elevation of privilege | rel-005 | Token tampered to claim a staff or manager role | Signed token, role re-read from the database, short staff session | mitigated |
| thr-010 | Information disclosure | ctr-db | PII (names, phones, birth years) read from storage or backups | Encryption at rest, encrypted backups, least-privilege credentials, no direct access except break-glass with audit | mitigated |
| thr-011 | Tampering | ctr-db | Double booking or invalid status written by a faulty code path | Exclusion constraint and status check in the database, application user without DDL rights (ADR-0003) | mitigated |
| thr-012 | Elevation of privilege | rel-006 | Leaked database credentials | Secret store, rotation, network access only from the container service | mitigated |
| thr-013 | Information disclosure | rel-007 | Patient phone and appointment details sent to a third party | Only templated fields sent, TLS, provider in India region under a data-processing agreement (CON-004) | mitigated |
| thr-014 | Denial of service | rel-007 | SMS provider down: codes and reminders do not arrive | Retry with backoff, circuit breaker, queued messages, alert on lag, reception list of failed reminders (FR-033); sign-in dependency accepted | accepted (RSK-002) |
| thr-015 | Spoofing | rel-009 | Forged delivery receipt or fake STOP for a patient | HMAC signature check, reject unsigned, ignore unknown message ids | mitigated |
| thr-016 | Spoofing | rel-010 | Forged WhatsApp receipt hides a failure | HMAC signature check, ignore unknown message ids | mitigated |
| thr-017 | Denial of service | rel-009 | Webhook flood | Rate limit at the edge, cheap handler, idempotent on provider message id | mitigated |
| thr-018 | Tampering | rel-008 | Replayed receipt triggers a second SMS fallback | Fallback created once per reminder (unique key on original message), receipts idempotent | mitigated |

## 10. Quality tactics and capacity

| Scenario | Tactic | Applied to (element ids) |
|---|---|---|
| QA-001 | Index on dentist and time; slots computed for one clinic and one date range per request; pagination of "first available" (top 10); CDN for static files | cmp-scheduling, ctr-db, ctr-web |
| QA-002 | One transaction per booking; confirmation sent after commit, not in the request; connection pool | cmp-booking, cmp-messaging, ctr-db |
| QA-003 | Exclusion constraint (ADR-0003); on conflict, return next free slots; idempotency key on booking requests so a retry never books twice | ent-appointment, cmp-booking, rel-005 |
| QA-004 | Precomputed reminder rows (ADR-0009); claim with skip locked; 3 attempts with backoff; circuit breaker; nightly reconciliation; lag alert; idempotent webhooks | cmp-messaging, cmp-channel-adapters, ent-message, rel-007, rel-008, rel-009, rel-010 |
| QA-005 | 2 replicas in two zones, health checks and auto-restart, managed database with synchronous standby, provider outage degrades only messages | ctr-clinic-api, ctr-db |
| QA-006 | Stateless API behind a load balancer, scale 2–4 on CPU, third clinic is data, database sized for 3 years (below) | ctr-clinic-api, ctr-db, cmp-scheduling |
| QA-007 | RBAC with clinic scoping in each module, argon2id, lockouts, signed cookie session, per-role tests | cmp-identity, rel-005 |
| QA-008 | Erasure operation that anonymises person, account and messages; retention jobs | cmp-identity, ent-patient, ent-message |
| QA-009 | Template-only sending, STOP processed on receipt (effective at once), template ids in configuration | cmp-messaging, cmp-channel-adapters, rel-009 |
| QA-010 | Mobile-first screens, 44 px targets, booking in 5 screens after sign-in | ctr-web |
| QA-011 | Clinics, dentists, hours, treatments are rows edited in staff screens | cmp-scheduling, ent-clinic |
| QA-012 | Single English message catalogue; times stored in UTC and shown in IST | ctr-web, cmp-messaging |

Capacity estimate:
- Peak users: NFR-003 says 30 patients booking at once; design for 3× = 100. A booking user sends about one request every 3 s while choosing, so 100 ÷ 3 ≈ 33 requests/s. Add about 10 reception and manager sessions refreshing every 30 s ≈ 0.3 requests/s. Design peak ≈ 35 requests/s.
- Cost per request: 3–6 indexed queries at about 3 ms each ≈ 20 ms of work, so one process serves about 50 requests/s per CPU. Two replicas of 1 vCPU give about 100 requests/s, about 3× the design peak; the second replica is for availability (QA-005), not load.
- Payload: free-slot list for "first available" (10 slots) ≈ 2 KB; day view (6 dentist columns of about 16 appointments) ≈ 15 KB; both well inside 2 s on 4G.
- Data growth: 1,000 appointments a week × 52 = 52,000 a year. Appointment ≈ 0.5 KB plus about 3 history rows of 0.3 KB ≈ 1.4 KB, so ≈ 73 MB a year. Messages about 4 per appointment (code, confirmation, reminder, change) ≈ 208,000 rows of 0.6 KB ≈ 125 MB a year, kept 90 days, so about 30 MB live. At 1 year ≈ 0.2 GB, at 3 years ≈ 0.5 GB including indexes. A small database tier (2 vCPU, 4 GB RAM, 20 GB disk) is enough, scaling path: a larger tier.
- Messages: about 4,000 SMS or WhatsApp messages a week at 1,000 appointments, and a dispatcher cycle every 30 s is plenty; the clinic pays about 4,000 charges a week.

SLOs:
| SLI | Target | Window |
|---|---|---|
| Free slots returned within 2 s (server p95 ≤ 500 ms leaves time for 4G) | 95% of requests | per month |
| Booking confirmed within 3 s (server p95 ≤ 800 ms) | 95% of requests | per month |
| Online booking available (probe every minute: health, slot read) | 99.5% (about 3.6 hours down a month) | per month |
| Reception screens available 08:00–20:00 Monday to Saturday | 99.5% of those hours | per month |
| Reminders sent within 15 minutes of scheduled time | 99% | per month |
| Reminders sent for cancelled appointments | 0 | per month |

